{"event_id":"d5419b74-2599-4e16-a7d7-df92300fa0cd","event_type":"verdict","timestamp_ns":516512918688830,"wall_time_iso":"2026-10-15T20:14:55.219000+00:00","payload":{"step_id":"pick_u_brown","verdict":"non_conformant","claimed_outcome":null,"clause_results":[{"field":"u_orange_stationarity","comparison":"le","threshold":0.02,"observed":0.0247,"pass":false,"clause_id":null,"source":"instruction"},{"field":"missed_grasp","comparison":"le","threshold":0.02,"observed":0,"pass":false,"clause_id":null,"source":"instruction"}],"explanation":"During pick_u_brown, after the grasped phase, get_evidence over 1792095234e9-1792095237e9 (object_poses, u_brown and u_orange; frames, tcp) shows u_brown's pose frozen bit-for-bit at its original resting value (x=0.56857, y=-0.33363, z=0.039824) for the entire lift window, while tcp climbed from z=0.127 to z=0.245 over the same interval and u_orange's pose rose in lockstep with it, from z=0.0400 to z=0.1571, tracking the arm's ascent. stat_u_orange fired (0.0247 m > 0.02 m disturbance_tolerance_m) and slip_u_brown (relative_frame tcp<->u_brown) fired simultaneously (0.0251 m > 0.02 m grasp_slip_tolerance_m) -- consistent, not contradictory: u_brown is not moving with the gripper because the gripper did not close on u_brown. The physical event is a wrong-object grasp: the gripper closed on and is lifting u_orange, the neighbouring inactive part, instead of the commanded u_brown, which never left the bench. This is a missed grasp (gripper closed on an object other than the one the step calls for) and simultaneously a disturbance of u_orange, a part this step's object_ids did not name and which must stay put until its own pick step. Both are violations: the missed-grasp requirement (no contract clause; instruction effect \"must close on that part and not another\") and the undisturbed-objects invariant over u_orange (clause_id null, instruction-level, using raw_spec.tolerances.disturbance_tolerance_m=0.02 m as the only disturbance bound available in this task's registry). I did not treat this as a simple stray touch because the correlation is exact and sustained (u_orange's full 3D trajectory tracks tcp's ascent across 10+ samples while u_brown's pose never changes at all), which rules out a transient brush and supports a true wrong-object grasp."},"subscriber_count":2,"seq":19}
